  "sections": [
    {
      "section_id": "thermometer_section_1",
      "section_title": "Thermometer",
      "section_order": 1,
      "subsections": [
        {
          "subsection_id": "thermometer_subsection_1",
          "subsection_title": "1. Identification and definition",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "thermometer_group_1",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A clinical thermometer is an instrument used to measure body temperature. It helps identify fever, hypothermia and changes in temperature associated with infection, inflammation, environmental exposure and other conditions."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Typical OSCE instruction: “Identify the instrument, describe its types, demonstrate how to measure temperature, state the normal range, and discuss its precautions and limitations.”"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "thermometer_subsection_2",
          "subsection_title": "2. Types of clinical thermometers",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "thermometer_group_2",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/thermometer_01.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A. Digital contact thermometer"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Measures temperature using an electronic sensor."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Sites: Axilla, oral cavity or rectum, depending on the model and patient."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Advantages: Affordable, easy to use, and suitable for many clinical settings."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/thermometer_02.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "B. Tympanic thermometer"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Uses infrared radiation to estimate temperature from the tympanic membrane region."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Advantages: Rapid and convenient when correctly positioned."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Limitations: Technique, ear anatomy and cerumen can affect accuracy."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/thermometer_03.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "C. Temporal artery or non-contact infrared thermometer"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Estimates temperature from infrared emissions at the forehead or temporal region, depending on the device."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Advantages: Fast, convenient and useful for screening."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Limitations: Environmental temperature, sweat, distance and positioning may affect readings."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/thermometer_04.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "D. Glass clinical thermometer"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Traditional thermometers use a liquid column. Older models may contain mercury; modern alternatives use other liquids."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Limitations: Fragility, slower measurement and potential toxic exposure if a mercury-containing device breaks."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "thermometer_subsection_3",
          "subsection_title": "3. Parts and functions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "thermometer_group_3",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "For a typical digital thermometer:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Part",
                    "Function"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Temperature sensor",
                      "Detects temperature at the measurement site."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Probe or tip",
                      "Contacts the measurement site."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Display",
                      "Shows the measured temperature."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Power button",
                      "Switches the device on and off."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Battery",
                      "Supplies power."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Protective probe cover, if applicable",
                      "Helps maintain hygiene and prevent contamination."
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "For an infrared thermometer, the sensor detects infrared energy rather than requiring direct contact with the measurement site."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "thermometer_subsection_4",
          "subsection_title": "4. Uses and indications",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "thermometer_group_4",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Detecting fever in suspected infection.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "1."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Detecting hypothermia.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "2."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Monitoring temperature trends in hospitalized patients.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "3."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Assessing children with febrile illnesses.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "4."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Monitoring neonates, who are particularly vulnerable to temperature instability.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "5."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Monitoring patients during and after procedures.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "6."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Supporting assessment of sepsis and other acute illnesses.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "7."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Monitoring response to treatment when temperature trends are clinically relevant.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "8."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Clinical pearl: A normal temperature does not exclude serious infection or sepsis, especially in neonates, older adults and immunocompromised patients."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "thermometer_subsection_5",
          "subsection_title": "5. Normal body temperature and fever",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "thermometer_group_5",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Body temperature varies with age, time of day, activity, measurement site and device."
                },
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Measurement",
                    "General interpretation"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Around 36.0–37.5°C",
                      "Common range for many healthy adults, depending on site and time"
                    ],
                    [
                      "38.0°C or above",
                      "Commonly used fever threshold"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Below 35.0°C",
                      "Hypothermia"
                    ],
                    [
                      "40.0°C or above",
                      "Very high temperature requiring prompt clinical assessment"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "These ranges are guides rather than absolute rules. The measurement site matters, and readings from different sites should not be treated as perfectly interchangeable."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Important pediatric distinction: A temperature of 38.0°C or above in an infant younger than 3 months requires urgent medical assessment, even if the infant appears relatively well."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "thermometer_subsection_6",
          "subsection_title": "6. Advantages and disadvantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "thermometer_group_6",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Type",
                    "Advantages",
                    "Disadvantages"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Digital contact",
                      "Affordable, widely available, versatile",
                      "Requires contact and cleaning; measurement time varies"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Tympanic",
                      "Rapid, convenient",
                      "Incorrect positioning and ear factors affect accuracy"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Temporal/forehead infrared",
                      "Fast, often non-contact",
                      "Environmental conditions and technique can affect readings"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Glass thermometer",
                      "Does not require batteries; some types are reusable",
                      "Fragile, slower; mercury models pose a toxic exposure hazard"
                    ]
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "thermometer_subsection_7",
          "subsection_title": "7. How to measure temperature: OSCE demonstration",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "thermometer_group_7",
              "group_title": "General practical checklist",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "0 of 9 steps completed"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "1. Perform hand hygiene, introduce yourself, confirm the patient's identity, explain the procedure and obtain consent."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "2. Choose a thermometer appropriate for the patient's age, condition and intended measurement site."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "3. Inspect the device and ensure it is clean and functioning correctly."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "4. Select the measurement site according to the device instructions and clinical protocol."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "5. Position the thermometer correctly and wait for the reading or completion signal."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "6. Remove the device carefully and read the displayed temperature."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "7. Clean or discard the probe cover according to infection-control guidance."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "8. Document the temperature, measurement site, time and relevant clinical context."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "9. If the reading is unexpected, assess the patient and repeat or confirm it using an appropriate method."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "**A. Axillary temperature measurement**",
                  "style": "heading"
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/thermometer_05.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/thermometer_06.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Explain the procedure and ensure the axilla is accessible.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "1."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "If the axilla is wet, gently dry it.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "2."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Place the thermometer tip high in the centre of the axilla, directly against the skin.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "3."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Bring the arm firmly against the chest to maintain contact.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "4."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Wait for the device's completion signal or the manufacturer's recommended measurement time.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "5."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Remove and read the thermometer.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "6."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Record the result as an axillary temperature.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "7."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Advantages: Non-invasive, easy to perform and generally well tolerated by children."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Limitations: Axillary measurements may be less accurate and may read lower than core temperature. Do not automatically add a fixed correction value to the result."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "**B. Oral temperature measurement**",
                  "style": "heading"
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/thermometer_07.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "slideserve.com"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Check that oral measurement is suitable for the patient.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "1."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Position the probe in the appropriate sublingual pocket beneath the tongue.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "2."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Ask the patient to close the mouth around the thermometer without biting it.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "3."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Wait for the reading.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "4."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Remove, read and clean the device appropriately.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "5."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Avoid oral measurement when the patient cannot cooperate safely, has a reduced level of consciousness, is at risk of biting the device, or has relevant oral injuries or recent procedures. Follow device instructions regarding recent hot or cold drinks."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "**C. Tympanic temperature measurement**",
                  "style": "heading"
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/thermometer_08.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "praxisdienst.com"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Select the correct probe cover if required.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "1."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Position the patient comfortably.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "2."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Gently straighten the ear canal as directed by the device instructions; in adults and older children, this commonly involves pulling the pinna upward and backward.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "3."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "In young children, the pinna is generally pulled backward and downward, according to the device and clinical protocol.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "4."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Insert the probe gently without forcing it.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "5."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Aim toward the tympanic membrane region and activate the device.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "6."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Remove and record the result.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "7."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Precaution: Do not force the probe into the ear, particularly in children or patients with ear pain, trauma or recent ear surgery."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "**D. Non-contact forehead temperature**",
                  "style": "heading"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Check the manufacturer's instructions for the required measurement distance.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "1."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Ensure the forehead is accessible and reasonably dry.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "2."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Avoid measuring over hair, head coverings or heavily perspiring skin.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "3."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Position the thermometer at the specified distance and angle.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "4."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Activate the device and record the reading.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "5."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "If the result is inconsistent with the clinical presentation, confirm it using an appropriate method.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "6."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "thermometer_subsection_8",
          "subsection_title": "8. Contraindications and precautions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "thermometer_group_8",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "There are few absolute contraindications to temperature measurement, but the measurement site must be selected safely."
                },
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Site",
                    "Important precautions"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Oral",
                      "Avoid in patients unable to cooperate, with reduced consciousness or significant risk of biting the thermometer."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Rectal",
                      "Avoid or seek specialist guidance in patients with relevant rectal disease, recent rectal surgery, significant bleeding risk or severe immunocompromise, according to local policy."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Tympanic",
                      "Use caution with ear trauma, pain, surgery or a probe that cannot be positioned safely."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Axillary",
                      "Ensure skin contact and adequate positioning; readings may be less representative of core temperature."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Forehead infrared",
                      "Account for sweat, environmental conditions and manufacturer-specified positioning."
                    ]
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "thermometer_group_9",
              "group_title": "Complications",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Cross-infection from inadequate cleaning.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Injury from inserting a probe forcefully.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Rectal trauma or bleeding from inappropriate rectal measurement.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Oral injury or device damage from biting.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Toxic exposure if a mercury-containing thermometer breaks.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Mercury safety: If a mercury thermometer breaks, do not vacuum or sweep up the mercury. Keep people away from the area and follow local hazardous-material or environmental health guidance."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "thermometer_subsection_9",
          "subsection_title": "9. Pediatric and neonatal considerations",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "thermometer_group_10",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/thermometer_09.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/thermometer_10.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Children require particular care because fever may be the first sign of serious infection, and neonates can become hypothermic rapidly."
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "title": "Key points:",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Choose an age-appropriate device and measurement site.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Avoid relying on touch alone to determine whether a child has fever.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "A digital thermometer used in the axilla is commonly practical in children.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Use rectal measurements only when appropriate for the clinical setting, trained personnel and local protocol.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Avoid oral thermometers in young children who cannot cooperate reliably.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Follow neonatal protocols for maintaining and monitoring body temperature.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Assess the child's general appearance, feeding, activity, respiratory effort and perfusion alongside the temperature.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "High-yield fact: In infants younger than 3 months, a measured temperature of 38.0°C or above is a high-risk finding that warrants urgent assessment for serious bacterial infection."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Hypothermia can also be a sign of serious illness in neonates; absence of fever does not provide reassurance."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "thermometer_subsection_10",
          "subsection_title": "10. Mnemonics and memory tips",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "thermometer_group_11",
              "group_title": "For temperature measurement: SITE",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "S — Select the appropriate site.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "I — Inspect the instrument.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "T — Take the measurement correctly.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "E — Enter the reading and site in the notes.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "thermometer_group_12",
              "group_title": "For a febrile young infant: “Under 3, 38 is urgent.”",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "An infant younger than 3 months with a temperature of 38.0°C or above needs urgent medical assessment."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "thermometer_subsection_11",
          "subsection_title": "11. Common OSCE questions and model answers",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "thermometer_group_13",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q1. What is a clinical thermometer used for?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It measures body temperature and helps identify fever, hypothermia and temperature trends."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q2. What is the commonly used fever threshold?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A temperature of 38.0°C or above is commonly used, although interpretation depends on the measurement site and clinical context."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q3. Why might an axillary reading differ from an oral or core temperature?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The axilla is a peripheral measurement site, and readings are influenced by skin contact, positioning, perfusion and the environment."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q4. What are the advantages of a tympanic thermometer?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It provides a rapid, convenient estimate of temperature and is generally well tolerated when used correctly."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q5. Why should mercury thermometers be avoided where safer alternatives are available?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Mercury is toxic, and breakage can cause hazardous exposure and environmental contamination."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q6. What would you do if a 6-week-old infant has a temperature of 38.2°C?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Arrange urgent medical assessment, assess airway, breathing, circulation and general appearance, and follow the local young-infant fever pathway. Do not delay assessment while repeating measurements unnecessarily."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q7. Can a normal temperature exclude sepsis?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "No. Serious infection can occur without fever, particularly in neonates and vulnerable patients."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "thermometer_subsection_12",
          "subsection_title": "12. Common mistakes that lose OSCE marks",
          "subsection_icon": null,
